  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1145 AM MST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\nThe upper level trough that brought us clouds and rain Friday\nnight into Saturday morning will continue to very slowly drift to\nthe southeast over northern Mexico. The trough will stay close\nenough to keep our skies cloudy through this evening. As the \ntrough slowly drifts to the southeast, we will see a slow clearing\ntrend from north to south late tonight into Sunday morning. By \nSunday afternoon most of the region will have clear skies except \nfor some lingering clouds around El Paso and Hudspeth Counties. \nHigh temperatures on Sunday will still run 2 to 8 degrees below \naverage. \n\nFor Monday and Tuesday, the upper level trough will continue to\nmove off to the east in Texas while an upper level ridge builds\ninto the west coast. This pattern will put our region in a dry\nnorthwest flow aloft. A northwest flow is usually a cool pattern so\nwe will continue to see our high temperatures running a few \ndegrees below average despite plenty of sun. For Wednesday through\nFriday, the west coast ridge will slowly build into the desert \nsouthwest. This ridge aloft will keep our skies clear and winds \nlight. High temperatures for the end of the work week will run \nnear or a few degrees above average. For next weekend, the models \nindicate that another upper level trough will approach the area to\ngive us unsettled weather for next weekend.",
